  "body": "MINOR `4.20.0` → `4.21.0`.\n\n## Problem\n\nThe update banner said *\"Update available: v4.20.0\"* and linked out. A version number alone doesn't tell you whether an update matters to you — so the part that actually decides it (what changed) sat one click away and mostly unread.\n\nThis landed badly in practice: v4.20.0 shipped three releases' worth of web search work, including a change that alters what the model sees and your token cost. Nothing in the dashboard conveyed that.\n\n## Change\n\nThe GitHub release feed already returns the notes in `body`; we simply never read them. `ReleaseStatus` now carries `release_notes`, and the banner renders a collapsed **What changed** section beneath the title.\n\nBounded at 4,000 characters with a trailing ellipsis — the feed is remote, so the banner shows an excerpt and keeps the existing link for the rest rather than rendering an unbounded blob into the dashboard.\n\n## Verification\n\nTests cover the notes reaching `ReleaseStatus` and `as_dict()`, a blank body yielding `None`, and the length bound.\n\nExercised against the **live feed**:\n\n```\ncurrent         : 4.18.2\nlatest          : 4.20.0\nupdate_available: True\nrelease_name    : v4.20.0 - Web search: full provider power, real error codes...\nnotes chars     : 4003\n```\n\n`ruff format`, `ruff check`, `ty check` clean; no suppressions. Full suite passes except the 14 failures that also fail on unmodified `main`.\n\n## Residual risks\n\nNotes render as pre-formatted text, not Markdown — headings and tables show their source characters. That's deliberate: rendering remote Markdown into the admin DOM is a larger call than this change should make on its own.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
